{"author":{"id":"746a4d30afc20b78119cb8ed7c194f988174fc252f0e9d9eb92022244ef86db3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1779150910,"nonce":"NMlzTt/H52RvGWPhjzMpiMjaeKA=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDOK4fCvM8AAAABCqS5yA","github-url":"https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/131","origin":"github"},"title":"gabby: apply sysctl UDP receive-buffer tuning to eliminate kernel-level drops","message":"## Goal\n\nEliminate the ~0.69% kernel-level UDP receive-buffer drops observed on\ngabby during the 2026-05-01 BizzyBoat deployment, by applying the\nsysctl tuning that the deployment log §9 already proposed but didn't\nland.\n\nPart of #124 (post-mission review §1.7).\n\n## What was observed (2026-05-01 deployment, gabby host)\n\nSnapshot at ~16:00 EDT during operations, ~2 h in\n(see [`docs/logs/2026/2026-05-01_gabby_logs.md` §9](docs/logs/2026/2026-05-01_gabby_logs.md)):\n\n```\nUdpInDatagrams      2,462,334\nUdpInErrors            17,023   (almost all RcvbufErrors)\nUdpRcvbufErrors        17,018   ≈ 0.69% of UDP receives dropped\nUdpSndbufErrors             0\n```\n\nNIC-level `rx_dropped`/`tx_dropped` were zero on both `enp7s0` and the\nZeroTier interface. The loss is at the **kernel → app socket-buffer\nhandoff**, after the NIC delivers. Send side is clean.\n\n## Why it's expected with current defaults\n\n| sysctl | current default | meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| `net.core.rmem_default` | 212,992 (208 KiB) | default UDP receive buffer for sockets that don't `setsockopt(SO_RCVBUF, ...)` |\n| `net.core.rmem_max` | 16,777,216 (16 MiB) | ceiling apps can claim — already big, not the constraint |\n| `net.ipv4.udp_rmem_min` | 4,096 (4 KiB) | per-socket guarantee under memory pressure |\n| `net.core.netdev_max_backlog` | 5,000 | per-CPU packet queue between NIC and protocol stack |\n\n`udp_bridge` and other UDP receivers don't ask for more, so they get the\n208 KiB default. Bursts (a topic flush, a keyframe being deserialized, a\ndiagnostics array) overflow that.\n\n## Proposed sysctl drop-in (verbatim from gabby log §9)\n\n`/etc/sysctl.d/31-udp-buffers.conf` — lexically separate from the\nexisting `30-starlink-tcp.conf`:\n\n```\nnet.core.rmem_default       4194304   # 4 MiB default (up from 208 KiB)\nnet.ipv4.udp_rmem_min        131072   # 128 KiB pressure floor (up from 4 KiB)\nnet.core.netdev_max_backlog  100000   # RX backlog before NAPI processes\nnet.core.netdev_budget          600   # only if softnet_stat shows budget exhaustion\n```\n\nTakes effect for new sockets after `sysctl -p`. Existing sockets keep\ntheir already-allocated buffer until the process reopens — so a\n`udp_bridge` restart picks up the new defaults; no host reboot needed.\n\n## Tracking decision (needs operator input)\n\n`30-starlink-tcp.conf` is **not** version-controlled today by precedent\n(per 2026-04-27 gabby log: *\"there is no precedent for tracking host\nsysctls in any layer repo\"*). Two options for this issue:\n\n1. **Match the existing precedent** — hand-apply on gabby, keep the\n   content quoted in the gabby log as the canonical reference. Simple,\n   matches what's already in place for TCP.\n2. **Start version-controlling host sysctls** — add a\n   `host_configs/gabby/sysctl.d/` (or similar) directory to one of the\n   relevant repos and check both `30-starlink-tcp.conf` and the new\n   `31-udp-buffers.conf` in. Cleaner long-term but is the bigger\n   \"should we be doing this everywhere\" change the 2026-04-27 log\n   explicitly punted on.\n\nDefault in this issue is (1) unless the operator decides otherwise.\n\n## Verification protocol (verbatim from gabby log §9)\n\nRun during the next deployment, after the boat is in the water with\nreal traffic flowing:\n\n```\nnstat -rsz UdpRcvbufErrors UdpInDatagrams\nsleep 60\nnstat -rsz UdpRcvbufErrors UdpInDatagrams       # delta — should be 0\n```\n\nIf drops stay at 0 over ~5 min of in-water traffic, the tuning landed.\nIf they don't, the bottleneck is downstream (app draining too slowly,\nwhich would point to a follow-up `setsockopt(SO_RCVBUF, ...)` change in\n`udp_bridge` itself — see \"Companion follow-up\" below).\n\n## Companion follow-up (separate work — flag, don't bundle)\n\nThe same gabby log §9 entry notes that `udp_bridge` (and any other UDP\nreceiver in the stack) should `setsockopt(SO_RCVBUF, ...)` to a large\nvalue explicitly, rather than rely on the system default. That's an\napplication-level code change, lower priority than the sysctl since\nthe sysctl helps every UDP socket on the box (not just `udp_bridge`).\nFile separately against [`rolker/udp_bridge`](https://github.com/rolker/udp_bridge)\nif/when the sysctl alone doesn't get drops to 0.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] `/etc/sysctl.d/31-udp-buffers.conf` applied on gabby with the\n      four lines above; `sysctl -p` run; `udp_bridge` restarted.\n- [ ] Tracking decision made (1 or 2 above) and reflected in either\n      the gabby log or a new host_configs commit.\n- [ ] Verification protocol run during the next deployment;\n      `UdpRcvbufErrors` delta = 0 (or, if non-zero, application-level\n      follow-up filed against `udp_bridge`).\n- [ ] Results documented in the next deployment's gabby log under a\n      new §9 entry (or wherever the log convention lands by then).\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- `udp_bridge` setsockopt code change (separate issue, conditional on\n  verification result).\n- Replicating to mercat / salmon / other Starlink-fronted hosts (those\n  hosts have different workloads and should be evaluated separately).\n- Broader host-config-tracking question — only revisit if option (2)\n  is taken in this issue.\n\n---\n**Authored-By**: `Claude Code Agent`\n**Model**: `Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context)`","files":null}]}